+\subsection{Ordinary and chart midpoint on the same dynamics}
+\label{gn:methods}
+The chart map in \eqref{struct:chart-midpoint} is tested without changing the magnetic model,
+its identified coefficients or the restoration controller. Three magnetic
+fields are considered separately: the uncompensated plant, the known
+fixed-error closed field, and the autonomous conservative $h_0$.
+Within each comparison the initial state, time interval and independent
+reference are identical for every method. Ordinary midpoint is compared
+with the direct weighted equations and an independently implemented
+time-dependent chart midpoint. In the proportional case the chosen chart
+is $\chi_\tau(X,Y)=(X,e^{\gamma\tau}Y)$; substitution gives exactly
+\eqref{struct:weighted-midpoint} in dimensionless variables. This
+equivalence uses the specified chart and its time-midpoint evaluation,
+rather than merely agreement of the observed orders. Central4 is included
+only for the same autonomous conservative $h_0$.
+
+A separate example tests configuration-dependent mass and nonproportional
+damping. Its known nonlinear canonical map and prescribed matrices are
+artificial mathematical data from the general construction, not material
+properties of the catheter. The ordinary and chart maps approximate the
+same field within each uncontrolled or controlled case. The controlled
+example retains nonproportional damping and is tested against its own
+$\mathcal K_u$ as well as the original $\mathcal K$. Supplementary
+Section~\ref{gsn:protocol} specifies the full fields, initial states,
+references, residuals and cost accounting.
+
+The independently implemented proportional chart and weighted trajectories
+agree to at most $1.907\times10^{-13}$ over all tested grids.
+The final ordinary-midpoint endpoint orders range from $1.99936$
+to $1.99953$; the conservative Central4 order is $3.99950$.
+The chart preserves its specified ideal structure to rounding-scale
+defects, whereas ordinary midpoint has a nonzero defect for the
+damped cases. Table~\ref{gn:comparison-table} gives representative
+finest-grid results; full series and costs are retained in the supplement
+and JSON. For the controlled nonproportional example, the finest-grid
+chart defect is $2.751\times10^{-15}$ against $\mathcal K_u$ but $7.626\times10^{-3}$
+against the uncontrolled $\mathcal K$. Thus rebuilding the closed-loop
+structure has a testable consequence; it is not a claim that the
+original structure survives arbitrary feedback.
